-- Passage rg12-connections-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('61d06bc3-f6c7-5207-83b7-199fe7ce9908', 'rg12-connections-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Blue That Came Too Late$q$, $q$For almost a century, the painting known as Harbor at Dusk hung in the fictional Kessridge Museum under the name of Amadou Ferrant, a celebrated painter who died in 1791. Visitors admired its deep blue sky, and the museum's guidebook called it one of Ferrant's finest late works.

In 2014, a conservator named Yuna Takahashi-Brandt began cleaning the painting, whose varnish had yellowed. As part of the routine, she removed a speck of paint from the edge of the sky for analysis. The result surprised her. The blue was not the costly mineral pigment Ferrant was known to use but a synthetic blue that, according to the museum's own reference records, was first manufactured in 1824, more than thirty years after his death.

Takahashi-Brandt considered two explanations. The blue could belong to a later repair, painted over Ferrant's original sky by a restorer. Or the whole painting could be the work of someone else. To decide between them, she examined the canvas under infrared light, which can reveal the sketch beneath the paint. If the sky were only a repair, the sketch underneath should match Ferrant's known habits. Instead, the infrared images showed a sketch drawn with a ruler, a method that Ferrant, whose notebooks show freehand drawing throughout his career, never used.

The museum's curator, Lior Abadi, at first resisted the conclusion, noting that the frame bore a label from a respected dealer of the 1790s. Takahashi-Brandt then examined the frame and found that the label had been glued onto a newer backing board, so it could have been moved from another work. Faced with the pigment, the sketch and the reused label together, Abadi agreed to change the attribution.

The painting now hangs with a card naming its artist as unknown, active around 1830. Attendance at the gallery rose after a local newspaper reported the story, and the museum built a small exhibit around the case, showing visitors the paint sample, the infrared images and the label. Abadi later remarked that the painting had become more interesting as a puzzle than it had ever been as a Ferrant.$q$, 'draft')
